-- gate: known-leniency
-- defect: unresolved name `mysteryValue` (not a Sky/stdlib/local binding)
-- oracle: reject [E1001] naming error at canonicalisation
--
-- DOCUMENTED RESOLVER LENIENCY (accept-parity, NOT a ty soundness hole).
-- `import Sky.Core.Prelude exposing (..)` opens the `Basics` kernel pseudo-
-- module; the Rust resolver cannot statically ENUMERATE kernel exports, so it
-- resolves any bare unknown name to a lenient `Res::Kernel` ref rather than
-- emit `[E1001]` (resolve.rs `resolve_var` kernel-open fallback). This is a
-- deliberate accept-parity choice: tightening it means enumerating every
-- kernel export in the resolver, a change that touches every example's
-- `exposing (..)` import with its own over-eager-rejection risk. It is
-- ORTHOGONAL to the v0.7 self-host killer classes (nominal unification +
-- exhaustiveness + annotation-vs-body — all closed by this milestone). The
-- Haskell oracle rejects because it carries the full kernel-function tables.
-- This file is tracked as a known leniency, not counted against the hard gate.
module Main exposing (main)

import Sky.Core.Prelude exposing (..)
import Std.Log exposing (println)

main =
    println mysteryValue
